          "path": "_m/d/n/k/j/clinton-tennessee-wp/clinton-green-mcadoo-center-tn.jpg",
          "filename": "Clinton-green-mcadoo-center-tn1.jpg",
          "caption": "The Green McAdoo Cultural Center in Clinton, Tennessee, in the southeastern United States.  The cultural center occupies what was once the Green McAdoo School, which was a school for African-Americans before desegregation.  Opposition arose from local citizens, including the current vice-mayor, then a student.  In 1956 12 Green McAdoo students (represented by the statues) transferred to the all-white Clinton High School, becoming the first African-American students to desegregate a state-supported school in the South. In 2006, the school reopened as a cultural center and museum dedicated to preserving this legacy.  In 2005, the school was added to the National Register of Historic Places. \n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis is an image of a place or building that is listed on the National Register of Historic Places in the United States of America. Its reference number is 05001218 (Wikidata).",
          "alt": "The Green McAdoo Cultural Center in Clinton, Tennessee, in the southeastern United States.  The cultural center occupies what was once the Green McAdoo School, which was a school for African-Americans before desegregation.  Opposition arose from local citizens, including the current vice-mayor, then a student.  In 1956 12 Green McAdoo students (represented by the statues) transferred to the all-white Clinton High School, becoming the first African-American students to desegregate a state-supported school in the South. In 2006, the school reopened as a cultural center and museum dedicated to preserving this legacy.  In 2005, the school was added to the National Register of Historic Places. \n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis is an image of a place or building that is listed on the National Register of Historic Places in the United States of America. Its reference number is 05001218 (Wikidata).",
